  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA few showers remain possible this afternoon, mainly across the\nhigher terrain and southern coalfields with uncapped profiles,\nbut progressively drier air working in from the north will erode\nany remaining MLCAPE as we move through the late afternoon.\n\nRadiational cooling tonight will foster river valley fog across\nthe forecast area. However, mid-level clouds advancing from the\neast may limit fog depth and coverage across the southern\ncoalfields. \n\nHigh pressure and a dry northwesterly flow regime aloft will\npersist across the central Appalachians through early Wednesday \nbefore the ridge flattens overhead by midweek. Within this \nairmass, surface temperatures will reach daytime highs in the \nmid to upper 80s in lower elevations, running roughly 3 to 5 \ndegrees above seasonal normals but notably cooler than recent \nextremes.\n\nBelow an elevated mixed layer based near H700, stable low-level\nthermodynamic profiles with virtually no instability will \nensure dry and capped conditions continue through Wednesday \nmorning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAn upper-level trough traversing the northern tier toward Hudson\nBay will flatten the upper ridge mid-week, driving daytime\ntemperatures briefly back near 90 degrees on Wednesday. As the\nsystem moves east, it will drag a cold front south toward the\nOhio Valley late Wednesday, where it is expected to orient west-\nto-east directly across the forecast area on Thursday. \n\nPrecipitation chances increase over southeast Ohio and western\ncounties late Wednesday evening, with widespread showers and\nthunderstorms expanding on Thursday along the boundary. While\ndeep-layer shear is forecast to be modest to strong for early\nSeptember at 35 to 45KTs, severe weather potential remains \nconstrained by limited surface-based instability. High \natmospheric moisture content with precipitable water values of \n1.8 to 2.0 inches, combined with steering flow parallel to the \nboundary, raises the risk for training convection and high \nrainfall rates. A Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall covers \nmuch of the region for Thursday, though antecedent dry \nconditions over the coming days should help limit initial flash \nflooding vulnerability.",
